WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.830
An oral-history interview is not a transparent
window into the past.

2
00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.645
It is a historical source created in a
particular encounter.

3
00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:14.781
The narrator chooses what to remember and how
to tell it; the interviewer shapes the path

4
00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:19.766
through questions and reactions; the recording
medium and archive shape what later audiences

5
00:00:19.766 --> 00:00:20.533
can access.

6
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:25.815
Begin with the source itself: who
participated, when and where it was recorded,

7
00:00:25.815 --> 00:00:31.504
what language or languages were used, what the
project asked about, and what permissions

8
00:00:31.504 --> 00:00:32.317
govern use.

9
00:00:32.426 --> 00:00:35.761
A disciplined reading separates three layers.

10
00:00:35.761 --> 00:00:38.990
Testimony is what the narrator communicates.

11
00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:45.913
Context includes institutions, events, places,
and social relationships that make the account

12
00:00:45.913 --> 00:00:46.490
legible.

13
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:52.173
Interpretation is the argument a researcher
builds by relating the interview to other

14
00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:52.610
evidence.

15
00:00:52.610 --> 00:00:58.217
Keeping these layers visible prevents an
editor from presenting an inference as the

16
00:00:58.217 --> 00:00:59.511
narrator's own claim.

17
00:00:59.511 --> 00:01:01.985
It also preserves disagreement.

18
00:01:01.985 --> 00:01:08.811
Memory can be selective without being useless;
the way an event is remembered may itself

19
00:01:08.811 --> 00:01:11.542
reveal identity, community, or later change.

20
00:01:11.666 --> 00:01:16.785
For Deaf oral histories, access is not a final
technical add-on.

21
00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:21.357
Language, interpretation, captioning,
transcription, camera framing, and interface

22
00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:27.071
design affect what can be expressed and what
audiences receive.

23
00:01:27.071 --> 00:01:33.092
A transcript can support search and quotation,
but it does not replace the embodied and

24
00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:36.303
linguistic information in a signed or spoken
recording.

25
00:01:36.303 --> 00:01:42.571
Editors should document who translated or
interpreted, preserve the original recording,

26
00:01:42.571 --> 00:01:47.699
and avoid forcing culturally specific
expression into misleading written

27
00:01:47.699 --> 00:01:48.269
equivalents.

28
00:01:48.269 --> 00:01:53.113
Accessible presentation and historical
accuracy are intertwined.

29
00:01:53.234 --> 00:01:57.741
Corroboration does not mean treating the
narrator like a suspect.

30
00:01:57.741 --> 00:02:00.553
It means building a richer evidence field.

31
00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:07.209
Compare dates and names with club minutes,
newspapers, photographs, directories, policy

32
00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:09.629
documents, and other interviews.

33
00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:15.267
When sources conflict, record the difference
and ask what each source was positioned to

34
00:02:15.267 --> 00:02:15.670
know.

35
00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:21.587
A club record may document an official
decision; an interview may reveal how that

36
00:02:21.587 --> 00:02:25.814
decision felt, who was excluded, or how
community members adapted.

37
00:02:25.814 --> 00:02:28.952
Neither source automatically cancels the
other.

38
00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:34.421
Before publishing, confirm that use matches
the recorded permission and that names and

39
00:02:34.421 --> 00:02:36.893
sensitive material are handled as agreed.

40
00:02:36.893 --> 00:02:42.091
Provide accurate captions, a reviewed
transcript, descriptive context, and

41
00:02:42.091 --> 00:02:46.711
provenance that identifies the collection and
editorial choices.

42
00:02:46.711 --> 00:02:50.630
Do not simulate sign language or invent a
narrator's voice.

43
00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:56.919
Make it easy for audiences to locate the
original source and for participants or

44
00:02:56.919 --> 00:02:58.716
communities to raise corrections.

45
00:02:58.716 --> 00:03:05.693
Ethical oral history preserves agency while
enabling study; accessibility is part of that

46
00:03:05.693 --> 00:03:08.377
preservation, not separate from it.
